const PS3_PASSAGE_7 = `Researchers studying health behavior change have identified several theoretical models that predict when individuals will modify risk behaviors. The Health Belief Model (HBM) proposes that health behavior change is determined by: (1) perceived susceptibility — whether the individual believes they are at risk for the condition; (2) perceived severity — how serious the individual believes the condition to be; (3) perceived benefits — the anticipated advantages of taking preventive action; (4) perceived barriers — the costs, difficulties, or negative aspects of taking action; and (5) cues to action — external triggers (symptoms, health campaigns, advice from physicians) that prompt health-protective behavior.

Subsequent models incorporated additional constructs. The Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB, Ajzen) proposes that the best predictor of actual behavior is behavioral intention, which is in turn determined by: (1) attitude toward the behavior (a positive or negative evaluation of performing it); (2) subjective norms (perceived social pressure to perform or not perform the behavior); and (3) perceived behavioral control (the perceived ease or difficulty of performing the behavior, similar to Bandura's self-efficacy).

The Transtheoretical Model (TTM, Prochaska) conceptualizes behavior change as proceeding through stages: precontemplation (no awareness or intention to change), contemplation (aware of the problem, considering change within 6 months), preparation (intending to change within 30 days, taking small steps), action (actively modifying behavior), and maintenance (sustaining the behavior change for 6+ months, preventing relapse).`

const PS3_PASSAGE_8 = `Poverty and social inequality intersect with health in complex ways that sociologists and epidemiologists have studied extensively. Social epidemiologist Michael Marmot conducted the Whitehall studies — longitudinal studies of British civil servants — and found a social gradient in health: each step down in the occupational hierarchy was associated with progressively worse health outcomes, even among people who were not poor in any absolute sense. Importantly, cardiovascular disease mortality increased monotonically with decreasing employment grade, even after controlling for traditional risk factors (smoking, diet, exercise).

Marmot concluded that psychosocial factors — particularly low control over one's work environment, lack of social support, and status anxiety — explained much of the gradient. Low job control was especially strongly associated with cardiovascular disease. This extended beyond physical risk factors and pointed to social position itself as a determinant of biological health.

William Julius Wilson's research on urban poverty highlighted the phenomenon of "concentrated poverty" — when the most economically disadvantaged individuals are geographically concentrated in neighborhoods lacking resources (good schools, safe environments, employment opportunities, healthcare access). He argued that the social isolation of the urban poor from mainstream employment networks and institutions created self-reinforcing cycles of disadvantage that were qualitatively different from dispersed poverty.`

const PS3_PASSAGE_9 = `Prejudice and discrimination have psychological costs for targeted groups beyond the direct effects of discriminatory treatment. Claude Steele's stereotype threat research demonstrated that reminding individuals of a negative stereotype about their group before a performance task impairs performance on that task, even among highly capable individuals. The effect requires only that the person care about performing well and be aware of the negative stereotype.

Social psychologists have identified several mechanisms underlying stereotype threat: (1) cognitive interference — intrusive thoughts about confirming the stereotype consume working memory resources; (2) physiological arousal — the threat triggers stress responses that impair performance; (3) identity threat — the relevance of the task to one's self-concept amplifies the effect; and (4) self-monitoring — increased vigilance about one's performance consumes attentional resources.

Research on implicit bias (Greenwald's Implicit Association Test, IAT) has shown that people can harbor automatic, unconscious associations between social groups and evaluative concepts (positive/negative, capable/incapable) even when they explicitly endorse egalitarian beliefs. These implicit associations can influence behavior in subtle ways — in hiring decisions, clinical recommendations, and social interactions — without the actor being aware of the influence.`

